[84.0094, "o", "[approval] bash: apt-get install -y -q python3-pip >/tmp/aptlog 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"; tail -5 /tmp/aptlog; echo \"=== verify 3.11\"; /usr/bin/python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 | tail -2 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash apt-get install -y -q python3-pip >/tmp/aptlog 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"; t…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Both interpreters now work. Final end-to-end check on both paths:\r\n[approval] bash: rm -f /tmp/aptlog; echo \"=== python3 (3.13, primary)\"; python3 -m pip --version; python3 -m pip install --root-user-action=ignore --dry-run -q six >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"dry-run exit=$?\"; echo; echo \"=== /usr/bin/python3 (3.11, distro)\"; /usr/bin/python3 -m pip --version; echo \"=== console scripts\"; pip --version; pip3 --version — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -f /tmp/aptlog; echo \"=== python3 (3.13, primary)\"; python3 -m pip …\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\nBoth are fixed and verified.\r\n\r\n## What was wrong\r\n\r\nNot a config or perm"]
[84.009436, "o", "issions problem — a **half-deleted install**. In `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`, pip's `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory was still on disk, but the actual `pip/` package directory it describes was gone. So:\r\n\r\n- `pip`/`pip3` existed at `/usr/local/bin` as scripts that begin `from pip._internal.cli.main import main`\r\n- that import failed with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`\r\n- and `python3 -m pip` failed the same way, since the module genuinely wasn't installed\r\n\r\nThe leftover metadata also made the obvious repair fail: `python3 -m ensurepip` reported *\"Requirement already satisfied: pip … (24.3.1)\"* and exited 0 without reinstalling anything, because it only looked at `dist-info` and never tried the import.\r\n\r\n## What I did\r\n\r\n1. Backed up and removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` so ensurepip would stop being satisfied by it.\r\n2. Ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip`, which reinstalled pip 24.3.1 from Python's own bundled wheel (no network needed).\r\n3. Fou"]
[84.009446, "o", "nd a second interpreter, `/usr/bin/python3` (Debian 3.11), which had never had pip and has no `ensurepip`; installed it through the packaging system with `apt-get install python3-pip` (23.0.1), rather than forcing a system install against PEP 668.\r\n4. Cleaned up the backup and test artifacts.\r\n\r\n## Check\r\n\r\n`python3 -m pip --version`, `pip --version` and `pip3 --version` all report 24.3.1 (3.13); `/usr/bin/python3 -m pip --version` reports 23.0.1 (3.11). A real `pip install --target` of `six` completed and imported, and a dry-run resolution now exits 0.\r\n\r\nTwo things worth knowing:\r\n\r\n- `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` is present, so **package installs into the 3.11 distro Python will still refuse** with an \"externally-managed-environment\" error. That's PEP 668 working as intended, not the bug you hit — use `--user`/a venv there, or `apt` for system packages. The 3.13 install has no such marker.\r\n- Your `PATH` puts `/usr/local/bin` ahead of `/usr/bin`, so bare `pip` is the 3.13 one. If you were prev"]
[84.00951, "o", "iously seeing behaviour from the 3.11 one, that's the difference.\r\n\r\nNothing was written to the repository — the fault was in the system Python, and I left the repo untouched.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=17 surface=59338fa2ec97\r\n"]
